<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lipitsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1216">1216</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lipitsa was fought in April 1216 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuryev-polsky">Yuryev-Polsky</a> between the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-of-rostov">Konstantin of Rostov</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav the Bold</a> of Novgorod, defeated his brothers Yuri and Yaroslav, whose army suffered heavy losses. The victory made Konstantin grand prince of Vladimir, and the battle is remembered as one of the bloodiest fought between Rus' princes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war of succession broke out after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vsevolod-the-big-nest">Vsevolod the Big Nest</a> died on 15 April 1212 and his sons fought over his inheritance. Its precise causes are unknown: the 16th-century Nikon Chronicle claims that Vsevolod disinherited his eldest son Konstantin in favour of Yuri, but the older and more reliable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurentian-codex">Laurentian Codex</a> does not mention this. After the fall and death in 1212 of the Kievan grand prince Vsevolod Chermnyi, Yuri's father-in-law, the brothers were excluded from the Kievan succession and turned on each other.</p><p>They also sought to regain control of the Novgorod Republic, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich">Mstislav Mstislavich</a> reigned from 1210 to 1215. Yaroslav briefly occupied Novgorod in 1215, and Mstislav allied himself with Konstantin. According to the Novgorodian First Chronicle, Mstislav set out against his son-in-law Yaroslav on 1 March 1216. Before the battle he offered peace if Yaroslav returned his Novgorodian districts, and asked that the Novgorodians in Yaroslav's army be sent home so that brothers would not fight brothers, but he was refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The chronicles do not give the order of battle; the account was written by a monk who was not familiar with strategy or tactics. They tell that the Novgorodians chose to fight on foot, as their fathers had done in an earlier battle, threw off their boots and ran into battle barefoot. With what the chronicle calls the help of Saint Sophia they drove off Yaroslav and Yuri, and the number of the dead was said to be countless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Yaroslav fled to Pereiaslavl, where he threw the Novgorodians of his army into a pit or prison, and many of them died. Yuri agreed to withdraw, and Mstislav placed Konstantin on the throne at Vladimir before returning to Novgorod. Konstantin reigned as grand prince until his death two years later.</p><p>The site of the battle was disputed until 1808, when a peasant woman from Lykovo near Yuriev-Polsky found a gilded helmet bearing an image of Saint Theodore, patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a>. It is usually attributed to Yaroslav, whom the chronicler describes fleeing the field without his armour, and it is preserved in the Kremlin Armoury.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lipitsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Internal wars proved as costly as foreign ones: at the <b>Battle of Lipitsa</b> in 1216 Mstislav the Bold of Novgorod and Konstantin of Rostov routed the brothers Yuri and Yaroslav, with heavy losses on the Suzdalian side.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
